<p style="text-align: center;"><img src="/images/2015/Furnace-Mountain.jpg" alt="A painting of hay bales in a field at Furnace Mountain in VA" width="550" height="396" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;">Hay Bales by Furnace Mountain. 9x12, oil on linen panel. 2015</p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">On the way home from a paint out last fall I stopped at the Point of Rocks boat ramp. Although nothing about the river caught my eye, on the way out I noticed a mowed field by the side of the ramp access road and felt it had some potential.</span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">To make the scene more interesting I changed reality and made the space much deeper - I've never seen a distant treeline in a painting and thought it was too far back.</span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">Hay bales are a staple of the landscape painter's&nbsp;</span>repertoire<span style="line-height: 1.42857143;">&nbsp;and art shows are always full of them so I try to limit myself to one a year. This painting may get a few touch-ups once it is dry.</span></p>
